# Target C-II — one complete deep-braid partner lane is necessarily nested

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only sharpening of the partner-rainbow theorem; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the endpoint-disjoint exact-antipodal deep-force braid on one pre-hub high-`t` shore through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** partner-rainbow support-order theorem; one-sided common-hub interval support; parameterized deep grid-force density; global one-sided reaction budget  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_nested_partner_support_rail_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The partner-rainbow theorem proves that the hubward partner-region word

\[
(b_M^+,\ldots,b_1^+,\;b_1^-,\ldots,b_M^-)
\tag{NR.1}
\]

is weakly unimodal. It records that at least one birth-side lane is monotone, leaving a nested-rainbow versus advancing-staircase dichotomy for that chosen lane.

The orientation of the two blocks removes that dichotomy. Because the `+` block is written in **reverse interface order** while the `-` block is written in **forward interface order**, weak unimodality forces one natural interface-order lane to have nonincreasing support ends. Hence one entire birth-side lane is always a nested support rainbow; no further thinning is required.

No assertion about which physical hubward host arc contains the partner endpoints is needed.

---

## Theorem NR — complete nested partner rail

Fix a disjoint deep braid with selected centers

\[
i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_M<k,
\tag{NR.2}
\]

and birth-side support-end tags

\[
b_s^\pm\ge k,
\qquad
I_s^\pm=\{i_s+1,\ldots,b_s^\pm\}.
\tag{NR.3}
\]

Then there is one fixed birth side

\[
\sigma\in\{+,-\}
\tag{NR.4}
\]

such that

\[
\boxed{
b_1^\sigma\ge b_2^\sigma\ge\cdots\ge b_M^\sigma.}
\tag{NR.5}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
I_1^\sigma\supsetneq I_2^\sigma\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_M^\sigma.
}
\tag{NR.6}
\]

Thus one selected diameter bar from **every** force cell forms a complete nested support rail of size `M`. All of these bars retain pairwise disjoint endpoints and pairwise geometric crossing from the parent deep braid.

### Proof

The partner-rainbow theorem gives the weakly unimodal sequence

\[
S=
(A\,|\,B)
:=
(b_M^+,\ldots,b_1^+\,|\,b_1^-,\ldots,b_M^-).
\tag{NR.7}
\]

For any weakly unimodal sequence split into two contiguous blocks `A|B`,

\[
\boxed{
A\text{ is weakly nondecreasing}
\quad\text{or}\quad
B\text{ is weakly nonincreasing}.}
\tag{NR.8}
\]

Indeed, if `A` is not nondecreasing, a strict descent already occurs inside `A`; a unimodal sequence can never ascend after its first descent, so all of `B` is nonincreasing. Conversely, if `B` is not nonincreasing, a strict ascent occurs inside `B`; no descent can have occurred earlier, so all of `A` is nondecreasing. Plateaux cause no problem.

Apply (NR.8) to (NR.7).

- If `A=(b_M^+,\ldots,b_1^+)` is nondecreasing in the displayed order, reversing the block gives
  \[
  b_1^+\ge b_2^+\ge\cdots\ge b_M^+.
  \tag{NR.9}
  \]
- If `B=(b_1^-,\ldots,b_M^-)` is nonincreasing, then directly
  \[
  b_1^-\ge b_2^-\ge\cdots\ge b_M^-.
  \tag{NR.10}
  \]

This proves (NR.5).

Finally `i_1<...<i_M` while the chosen `b_s` are nonincreasing and all satisfy `b_s>=k>i_M`. Therefore

\[
\{i_{s+1}+1,\ldots,b_{s+1}\}
\subsetneq
\{i_s+1,\ldots,b_s\}
\tag{NR.11}
\]

for every `s`, proving (NR.6). `square`

---

## 1. Coherent transverse-sign nested subrail

The nested birth-side rail chooses exactly one of the two opposite-transverse force bars at each selected cell. Relative to the coherently oriented high-`t` row axes, color each rail bar by the sign of its transverse derivative. One color occurs on at least half the cells.

Hence there is a subrail `L_D` with

\[
|L_D|\ge\left\lceil{M\over2}\right\rceil
\tag{NR.12}
\]

that simultaneously has:

- strictly nested laminar support intervals;
- pairwise disjoint source endpoints;
- pairwise geometric crossing;
- one common transverse sign;
- pairwise disjoint deep exact-phase neighborhoods;
- the common inverse-`D` near-triangular train grid;
- the fixed local two-sided cusp inherited from every force cell.

For a parameterized parent density

\[
\lambda=c_0-6\rho-\eta-2\delta>0,
\qquad
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000},
\tag{NR.13}
\]

the growing-depth conflict extraction gives

\[
M>
\left({\lambda\over16\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4},
\tag{NR.14}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
|L_D|>
\left({\lambda\over32\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4}.
}
\tag{NR.15}
\]

The point is stronger than a generic monotone-support lane: the coherent-sign lane may be chosen **inside a nested support rainbow**.

---

## 2. Stress-dilute nested rail

Impose the global KKT quantile before the endpoint/window conflict extraction. For fixed `0<sigma<1`, delete centers with

\[
B_i>{1\over\sigma\lambda D}.
\tag{NR.16}
\]

The one-sided reaction budget leaves

\[
>(1-\sigma)\lambda D-o(D)
\tag{NR.17}
\]

parent cells satisfying

\[
\boxed{
B_i\le{1\over\sigma\lambda D},
\qquad
0<|G_i|\le{1\over\sigma\lambda}.
}
\tag{NR.18}
\]

Apply the same endpoint/window conflict extraction and Theorem NR. On the growing-depth diagonal `r_D->infinity`, `r_D=o(D^(3/4))`, one obtains a **nested** rail of size

\[
\boxed{
M_D^{budget}>
\left({(1-\sigma)\lambda\over16\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4},
}
\tag{NR.19}
\]

with (NR.18) at every birth cell. A coherent-sign nested subrail has half this leading coefficient:

\[
\boxed{
|L_D^{budget}|>
\left({(1-\sigma)\lambda\over32\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4}.
}
\tag{NR.20}
\]

The exact compressed force coefficients also retain

\[
0<\lambda_i^\pm
\le {1\over\sigma\lambda D\sin\rho}.
\tag{NR.21}
\]

Thus nesting, coherent transverse sign, growing exact-phase depth, and `O(D^-1)` per-cell switching stress can all be imposed simultaneously.

---

## 3. Explicit current constants

Use

\[
\rho=\delta={c_0\over100},
\qquad
\eta={c_0\over50},
\qquad
\lambda={9c_0\over10},
\qquad
\sigma={1\over2}.
\tag{NR.22}
\]

Then the stress-dilute nested rail has

\[
\boxed{
M_D^{budget}>
(1.38516280797\times10^{-7}-o(1))D^{1/4},
}
\tag{NR.23}
\]

and every retained cell satisfies

\[
B_i\le{393313.668\over D},
\qquad
0<|G_i|\le393313.668.
\tag{NR.24}
\]

After imposing one common transverse sign,

\[
\boxed{
|L_D^{budget}|>
(6.92581403985\times10^{-8}-o(1))D^{1/4}.
}
\tag{NR.25}
\]

Without the stress-quantile loss, the current ninety-percent parent density gives coherent-sign nested coefficient

\[
1.38516280797\times10^{-7},
\tag{NR.26}
\]

and the parameter limit permits this coefficient to approach

\[
1.53906978663\times10^{-7}.
\tag{NR.27}
\]

